{"birds": [{"name": "American Goldfinch", "sci": "Spinus tristis", "n": 2, "first": "8:59 AM", "last": "9:18 AM", "ts": 1793192354, "mins": [539, 558], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "One of the strictest vegetarians among birds. Goldfinches nest late in summer, when thistle seeds are ripe, and swap bright yellow for olive-brown in winter.", "vox": "sounds/voice/spinus-tristis.mp3?v=1793113053", "snd": "sounds/spinus-tristis.mp3?v=1793113090", "sndby": "Will Sweet, xeno-canto XC453934, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "goldfinch", "cm": 12.0, "img": "cutouts/goldfinch.png", "w": 517, "h": 369, "len": 415.0, "beak": [0.18, 0.15, "L"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 23, "b": "APgAAAH4AAAD/AAAB/wAAAP+AAEB/wAHAf+ADgH/wBwB/8DwAf/h4AH/94AA//8AAH/8ABA/+AAAH/wAAB/+AAA/fhAAeA8AA/gHgAfgB8APAANAPAAAADgAAAA="}}, {"name": "Carolina Chickadee", "sci": "Poecile carolinensis", "n": 8, "first": "8:59 AM", "last": "9:08 AM", "ts": 1793192356, "mins": [539, 541, 542, 543, 543, 545, 547, 548], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Chickadees hide seeds in many separate spots and can remember where for weeks, then dig them back out when food gets scarce.", "vox": "sounds/voice/poecile-carolinensis.mp3?v=1793113028", "snd": "sounds/poecile-carolinensis.mp3?v=1793113078", "sndby": "J.R. Rigby, xeno-canto XC309925, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "chickadee", "cm": 12.5, "img": "cutouts/chickadee.png", "w": 686, "h": 800, "len": 829.0, "beak": [0.78, 0.05, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 37, "b": "AAB+AQAA/8MAAf+DAAP/gwAD/wYAA/8GAAf/PAAH/7wAB//8AA//+AAf//4AH/+/AD//vwA//38Af/98AP//5AD//sAA//3AAf/9gAH//4AD//+AA///gAf//4APg/wADgH8ABwA+AA4APgAeAH4AHAB8ADgA/AAwAeAAMAPAAAAHwAAADwAAAA8AAAAeAAAADAAAA=="}}, {"name": "House Finch", "sci": "Haemorhous mexicanus", "n": 9, "first": "8:59 AM", "last": "9:17 AM", "ts": 1793192388, "mins": [539, 542, 543, 547, 550, 550, 551, 555, 557], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Native to the West, house finches were released on Long Island in 1940 as pet-shop “Hollywood finches” and spread across the East. Redder males are favored by females.", "vox": "sounds/voice/haemorhous-mexicanus.mp3?v=1793113005", "snd": "sounds/haemorhous-mexicanus.mp3?v=1793113108", "sndby": "Ted Floyd, xeno-canto XC622745, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "housefinch", "cm": 14.0, "img": "cutouts/housefinch.png", "w": 800, "h": 581, "len": 662.0, "beak": [0.12, 0.13, "L"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 23, "b": "B+AAAAfwAQoP8AHOH/gB7wf8Af8D/gH/B/8A3wf/AP4H/4zgB//fwAP/zwAD//4AAf/+AAD//AAAf/gAAD/8AA/+/AAH4B4AB+APAB6AD4A4AAeA8AAAAMAAAAA="}}, {"name": "Blue Jay", "sci": "Cyanocitta cristata", "n": 4, "first": "9:00 AM", "last": "9:04 AM", "ts": 1793192409, "mins": [540, 541, 543, 544], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "That blue isn’t a pigment. Tiny structures in the feathers scatter light, which is why a crushed blue feather looks brown. Jays also bury acorns and help spread oak trees.", "vox": "sounds/voice/cyanocitta-cristata.mp3?v=1793112993", "snd": "sounds/cyanocitta-cristata.mp3?v=1793113328", "sndby": "Christopher McPherson, xeno-canto XC691163, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "bluejay", "cm": 28.0, "img": "cutouts/bluejay.png", "w": 385, "h": 369, "len": 474.0, "beak": [0.99, 0.17, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 31, "b": "AAAPgAAAD+AAAA/4AAAH+AAAD/8AAB//AAA//gAAf/AAAP/wAAH/8AAD//AAB//wAB//4AAf/+AAf//gAH//zAD//5wA//94Af+OcAP/8eAD9/fgA/l/gAPgHwAHgD4AHwA4AB8AeAA+AGAAfADgAHgAwADwAAAA4AAAAA=="}}, {"name": "Ruby-crowned Kinglet", "sci": "Corthylio calendula", "n": 2, "first": "9:01 AM", "last": "9:25 AM", "ts": 1793192515, "mins": [541, 565], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Its ruby crown stays hidden under olive feathers until the male gets excited, then it flares up. A ruby-crowned kinglet flicks its wings constantly.", "vox": "sounds/voice/corthylio-calendula.mp3?v=1793112988", "snd": "sounds/corthylio-calendula.mp3?v=1793113143", "sndby": "Andrew Spencer, xeno-canto XC104679, CC BY-NC-ND 2.5", "key": "rubykinglet", "cm": 10.8, "img": "cutouts/rubykinglet.png", "w": 800, "h": 653, "len": 568.0, "beak": [0.09, 0.35, "L"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 26, "b": "AAAAAwAAAAcAAAAGAAAADAAAADgAAAAwB8AAYA/wAOA//ADAH/8BwAf/hwAH/+YAB//8AAP//gAD//+AAf/jAAD/wAAAH4AAAD8AAAB+AAAA8AAAAfAAAA+AAAAeAAAAeAAAAPAAAAA="}}, {"name": "White-throated Sparrow", "sci": "Zonotrichia albicollis", "n": 9, "first": "9:04 AM", "last": "9:19 AM", "ts": 1793192686, "mins": [544, 550, 551, 552, 552, 553, 553, 558, 559], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "White-throats come in two color forms, white-striped and tan-striped, and almost always pair with the opposite form. Listen for “Oh-sweet-Canada, Canada, Canada.”", "vox": "sounds/voice/zonotrichia-albicollis.mp3?v=1793113071", "snd": "sounds/zonotrichia-albicollis.mp3?v=1793113140", "sndby": "Matt Wistrand, xeno-canto XC339648, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "whitethroated", "cm": 17.0, "img": "cutouts/whitethroated.png", "w": 800, "h": 460, "len": 599.0, "beak": [0.73, 0.14, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 18, "b": "AAB+AAAB/gAAB/8DAB/+BwB//h4B//54f//84P///8AAP/8AAA/8AAAD8AAAA/AAAA8AAAA/AAAAeAAAAeAAAAeAAAAHAAAA"}}, {"name": "Eastern Bluebird", "sci": "Sialia sialis", "n": 11, "first": "9:07 AM", "last": "9:25 AM", "ts": 1793192826, "mins": [547, 548, 548, 548, 549, 551, 557, 564, 565, 565, 565], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Bluebirds nest in tree cavities, and their numbers crashed in the early 1900s when starlings and house sparrows took the holes. Volunteer nest-box trails helped them rebound.", "vox": "sounds/voice/sialia-sialis.mp3?v=1793113046", "snd": "sounds/sialia-sialis.mp3?v=1793113130", "sndby": "Robin Carter, xeno-canto XC1374, CC BY-NC-ND 2.5", "key": "bluebird", "cm": 18.0, "img": "cutouts/bluebird.png", "w": 800, "h": 609, "len": 744.0, "beak": [0.69, 0.1, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 24, "b": "AAPwAAAH/AAAD/4AAA/4AAAf8AAAH/AAAD/wAAB/+AAAf/gDAP/wDgH/8BwD//B4A//h4Af/54AH/94AD/98AB//4AA/P4AAPH0AAHPhAABvgAAA3gAAAPgAAABwAAAA"}}, {"name": "Tufted Titmouse", "sci": "Baeolophus bicolor", "n": 9, "first": "9:09 AM", "last": "9:24 AM", "ts": 1793192969, "mins": [549, 549, 550, 551, 552, 562, 562, 563, 564], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Titmice line their nests with hair, and they have been seen plucking it from live raccoons, dogs, and even people.", "vox": "sounds/voice/baeolophus-bicolor.mp3?v=1793112971", "snd": "sounds/baeolophus-bicolor.mp3?v=1793113083", "sndby": "Jim Berry, xeno-canto XC649331, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "titmouse", "cm": 16.5, "img": "cutouts/titmouse.png", "w": 574, "h": 800, "len": 653.0, "beak": [0.2, 0.25, "L"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 45, "b": "cAAAAHAAAAAwAAAAIAAAAGgDAAB8B4AAPB8AAD5/AAA+/4AAeP+AAHv/wAB7/8AA+P/gAPhn4ACbH/AADz/wAA9/+AAPf/wABb/8AB+//gAO//4ABt//AAfP/wAHx/+AB+P/gAPh/8AHiP/AB47/wAeH/+AHofvgBvOB8AD3APgAfAB4APgAPAD4AB4A+AAfAPwADwA3AA8APwAHAD4AAAAfAAAAGwAAABgAAAAMAAAADAAA"}}, {"name": "Red-shouldered Hawk", "sci": "Buteo lineatus", "n": 21, "first": "9:11 AM", "last": "9:26 AM", "ts": 1793193060, "mins": [551, 554, 555, 555, 555, 556, 556, 557, 557, 558, 560, 560, 562, 563, 563, 563, 564, 564, 565, 565, 566], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Its loud, repeated kee-ah call is a favorite for Blue Jays to copy, so a hawk you hear in the yard may really be a jay.", "vox": "sounds/voice/buteo-lineatus.mp3?v=1793193504", "snd": "sounds/buteo-lineatus.mp3?v=1793193566", "sndby": "Denis Provencher, xeno-canto XC808846, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "life": true, "key": "redshouldered", "cm": 43.0, "img": "cutouts/redshouldered.png", "w": 523, "h": 800, "len": 863.0, "beak": [0.987, 0.093, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 49, "b": "AAAH+AAAD/4AAA/+AAAf/wAAP/8AAD//AAA/+AAAf/AAAP/4AAH/+AAB//gAA//4AAf/+AAP//gAH//8AD///AB///wAf//4AP//+AD///gB///wAf//8AP//+AD///gA///wAf//8AH//+AD///AA///gAP//4AH//8AB///AAf//wAH//4AB//+AA///gAP//4AH//8AD/9/gA/+f4AP/AewD/g//gPwf/4D4H/4B+B3+AfgAcAHwADAD8AAAAeAAAAA=="}}, {"name": "Carolina Wren", "sci": "Thryothorus ludovicianus", "n": 11, "first": "9:17 AM", "last": "9:22 AM", "ts": 1793193444, "mins": [557, 557, 558, 558, 559, 559, 560, 561, 561, 561, 562], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Its song sounds like “teakettle, teakettle, teakettle,” and pairs often sing duets. It will nest in odd places: flowerpots, mailboxes, even old boots.", "vox": "sounds/voice/thryothorus-ludovicianus.mp3?v=1793113060", "snd": "sounds/thryothorus-ludovicianus.mp3?v=1793113075", "sndby": "Jacob Saucier, xeno-canto XC498646, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "wren", "cm": 13.0, "img": "cutouts/wren.png", "w": 800, "h": 633, "len": 781.0, "beak": [0.85, 0.21, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 25, "b": "OAAAADgAAAA8AAAAHgB+AB4B/+APA//wB5//AAf//gAH//4AB//+AAf//AAH//wAD//4AAP/+AAB//H/AP/f/wA///8AGf/cAB/7AA//8AAP/4AAP/8AAP9gAAD8AAAAcAAAAA=="}}, {"name": "Fish Crow", "sci": "Corvus ossifragus", "n": 1, "first": "9:22 AM", "last": "9:22 AM", "ts": 1793193762, "mins": [562], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "It looks just like an American Crow. The giveaway is the voice: a nasal, two-note “uh-uh” instead of a clear “caw.”", "vox": "sounds/voice/corvus-ossifragus.mp3?v=1793112991", "snd": "sounds/corvus-ossifragus.mp3?v=1793113105", "sndby": "Mike Nelson, xeno-canto XC130581, CC BY-NC-SA 3.0", "key": "fishcrow", "cm": 38.0, "img": "cutouts/fishcrow.png", "w": 800, "h": 474, "len": 557.0, "beak": [0.27, 0.08, "L"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 19, "b": "AH4AAAD+AAAAPwAAAB+AAAAf4AAAH/AAAB/4AAAf/gAAD/4AAAf/AAAD/4AAAf/AAAD34P8Bs+D///Dg/////v////8AD///AAAP/w=="}}, {"name": "Cedar Waxwing", "sci": "Bombycilla cedrorum", "n": 1, "first": "9:22 AM", "last": "9:22 AM", "ts": 1793193766, "mins": [562], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "The red tips on its wing feathers look like drops of sealing wax, which gave waxwings their name. They sometimes pass berries beak to beak along a row of perched birds.", "vox": "sounds/voice/bombycilla-cedrorum.mp3?v=1793112972", "snd": "sounds/bombycilla-cedrorum.mp3?v=1793113168", "sndby": "Rory Nefdt, xeno-canto XC1142128, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "life": true, "key": "waxwing", "cm": 17.0, "img": "cutouts/waxwing.png", "w": 800, "h": 738, "len": 708.0, "beak": [0.61, 0.21, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 30, "b": "AAAADgAAAAwAAAAMAA8ADwAfwB8AD/AfAB/x2AAf4fgAP8H4AH+B/AD/xPwA/8X4Af/HgAP/hwAD/4YAB/+OAA//nAAP/zAAD/5wAB/9wAA//4AAP/8AAH/eAAA8+gAAeHAAAHDgAADj4AAAw4AAAEcAAAAGAAAA"}}], "also": [], "date": "Wednesday, October 28", "where": "", "updated": "9:26 AM", "sun": null, "code": "ee184552d3", "listen": {"state": "on", "since": null, "control": false}}